# Inactive v2 verifier — actual isolated SQL composition

Owned file: `src/lib/ai/conversation/__tests__/p5-voice-v2-authority-verifier-sql.test.ts` only.

This fixture uses the existing PGlite 0.5.12 / PostgreSQL 18.6 engine, all six preserved P3b migrations, the existing P5 SQL draft chain, actual protected parent/current transports, and real unchanged P3b AES-GCM/HMAC. All Auth, policies and approvals are fictional trusted-host inputs. The full current receipt must originate from actual isolated SQL; local response and timing hooks model between-await changes without claiming native concurrency or HTTP fault injection.

Seed state includes deliberate saved fictional text and unrelated same-owner accounting holds. A 22-table snapshot comparison allows only the tested owner's safe high-water clock update after verification; every other record is preserved except explicit fixture mutations, whose post-mutation baseline is captured before verification resumes. Original issuance time, expiry and fingerprint are actual persisted facts.

Coverage includes valid pre-claim verification, older issuance under fresh shorter Auth, changed or revoked exact metadata after the first protected current read, legacy fences, Auth owner/session change or absence, shrink-only deadline observations, late and malformed/missing replies, strict 29,999/30,000/30,001 ms original-age boundary, local rollback, short approval expiration during result wait, a new seal requiring its own immutable issuance, and real parent/approval changes during the last Auth wait. Only protected v2 reads are permitted in the verifier transport; setup registrars are not verifier calls.

No connected SQL/settings, real Auth/provider/device calls, lifecycle registration, claim/proof consumption, admission, capacity operation, publication or runtime wiring occurs. Single-connection SQL and local doubles do not establish cross-backend/native scheduling, provider finality, deployed Auth or device acceptance. Both engine instances are closed, Date mocks restored and original SQL clock function restored.

